# LICENSE — FOUNDER MUST CHOOSE (do not publish without this file)

This repository currently has NO license file. Until the founder picks one,
the code is "all rights reserved" by default — which blocks open-source use.
Pick ONE of the options below, then run the matching `cp` command.

## Option A — MIT (recommended for ecosystem growth)

A short permissive license: anyone may use, fork, modify and sell the code,
provided they keep the copyright notice. For an L1 this maximizes integrations
(wallets, exchanges, indexers) because there is zero legal friction — but it
also lets competitors run closed modified versions without sharing improvements.

```sh
cp ../stellereum/LICENSE-MIT ./LICENSE-MIT
```

## Option B — Apache-2.0 (recommended if patents matter)

Same permissiveness as MIT, plus an explicit patent grant and retaliation
clause: contributors grant patent rights to users, and anyone who sues over
those patents loses the grant. For an L1 with novel cryptography (PQ Falcon,
custom BFT) this is the safest permissive choice — it deters patent attacks
while keeping integration friction near zero. Note: the main `stellereum`
repo is already dual-licensed MIT/Apache-2.0; matching it keeps the
whole project uniform.

```sh
cp ../stellereum/LICENSE-APACHE ./LICENSE-APACHE
```

## Option C — GPL (copyleft: share-alike)

Anyone who distributes modified versions (including running a modified node
as a public network service — use AGPL for that) must publish their changes
under the same license. For an L1 this prevents closed forks and forces
improvements back upstream — but it scares off commercial integrators and is
incompatible with several exchange/wallet compliance policies.

```sh
# GPL text is NOT vendored locally; fetch it or pick via the GitHub web UI:
curl -o LICENSE https://www.gnu.org/licenses/gpl-3.0.txt
```

After choosing: delete this `LICENSE.TODO` file (`rm LICENSE.TODO`).
